"Introducing Mistral Large 4 Mistral AI has announced the public preview of Mistral Large 4 (ML4), its largest natively multimodal model with open weights to date. Mistral Large 4 is a native multimodal model featuring 52 billion active parameters out of 1 trillion total parameters. It delivers state-of-the-art performance across key enterprise workloads, including coding, agentic workflows, visual grounding, and cybersecurity. It was trained from scratch on 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs in its European data center, with weights scheduled for release later this month. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral has established a new hub in Munich, Germany, strengthening its industrial AI and Physics AI capabilities for Europe's largest industrial powerhouse. Mistral opens its Munich hub, deploying dedicated Physics AI and industrial AI research teams along with applied engineers to support key German enterprise partners. To secure European AI sovereignty, it supports running open-weight architecture-based models on on-premise infrastructure, with plans to build 1 gigawatt (1GW) of computing capacity within Europe by 2030. Leveraging talent from Emmi AI acquired in May 2026, it is laying out blueprints for Europe's heavy industry sector, including crash simulations with BMW and developing industrial AI applications with Siemens Energy. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral AI and Mozilla have partnered to introduce privacy-preserving, open, multilingual AI models to Firefox's AI browsing assistant. Mistral models will be integrated into Mozilla's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window (beta)'. It will be made available first to users in France and North America, with plans to expand to the UK and Germany later this year. Conversations are not saved on Mozilla's servers by default, and Mistral also adheres to a zero data retention policy to protect privacy. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral and Cloudera collaborate to support enterprises in building and deploying Sovereign AI while maintaining full control over their proprietary data and infrastructure. Integrating Mistral AI models with Cloudera's hybrid data platform to provide a secure inference environment across on-premises, public/private clouds, and fully air-gapped environments Enabling enterprises to train and build their own AI models based on decades of accumulated proprietary data, securing complete ownership over their data and intelligence Meeting the demand for Sovereign AI, which keeps data, computing, and overall model operations under customer control Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. A case study showing how Mistral AI utilized AI agents and a structured verification workflow to successfully migrate a complex 40,000-line Fortran 77 legacy simulator from a European energy company into a modern C++ system. Modernized a 40,000-line physics reservoir simulator—which completely lacked test suites and centralized documentation—into C++. Went beyond simple syntax translation to establish a numerical equivalence verification harness, documentation via AI agents, and a human-in-the-loop review framework. Refactored Fortran 77-specific limitations, such as global COMMON blocks and implicit typing, into an object-oriented architecture and integrated it with modern computing frameworks like PETSc. These execs think voice AI hasn’t reached its ChatGPT moment yet Despite massive investments continuing to pour into voice AI technology, industry executives assess that it has not yet reached its true 'ChatGPT moment.' Although full-duplex models have been developed, the challenge of drastically reducing inference speed to enable natural conversations remains. Meeting transcription service Otter sees digital twin technology—capable of speaker identification, intent recognition, and emotional expression—as a core challenge. Missed keywords and inaccurate transcriptions in automatic speech recognition (ASR) are cited as major limitations that undermine the reliability of downstream automation tasks. Learning to use local AI is exciting, overwhelming, and frustrating An exploration of the experiences and utility of running privacy-focused local AI models and agents directly on high-performance machines such as the Mac Studio instead of relying on cloud services. Interest in local AI that runs independently on desktops without handing over personal privacy data to cloud AI is surging rapidly. The open-source desktop app Hermes Agent was installed on an M5 Ultra Mac Studio equipped with 256GB of unified memory to run large local models. Among numerous models, the 125-billion-parameter (approx. 105GB) Qwen 3.8 Flash Next model was selected to begin testing, with plans to expand experiments to smaller models and various devices in the future. OrcaRouter Releases OrcaCyber Zero 1.5 Cybersecurity Model With 1M Context OrcaRouter has released OrcaCyber Zero 1.5, a cybersecurity model for authorized vulnerability research and penetration testing featuring a 1M token context window and native function calling capabilities. OrcaCyber Zero 1.5 is a post-trained model designed for vulnerability reproduction, exploit development, and penetration testing, supporting a 1M token context window and up to 128K maximum output tokens. According to vendor-reported benchmark results, it achieved 100% (39/39 tasks) on Cybench, 95.8% on CVE-Bench, and 76.5% on SWE-bench Pro V2. It is available exclusively via OrcaRouter's hosted API, priced at $3.00 for input and $7.50 for output per 1M tokens, restricted to vetted security research accounts. Middle School Student Proves 'Solid That Cannot Pass Through Itself' Conjecture with AI (Claude) A middle school student proved a mathematical conjecture regarding a 'solid that cannot pass through itself' using the AI model Claude. A middle school student succeeded in proving a geometric conjecture by utilizing AI (Claude) Demonstrates that AI can serve as a collaborative tool for complex mathematical research and problem-solving The detailed body of the article was not properly collected due to a security verification page Show GN: Haejwo Local - A Local AI Agent Orchestrator with a Credential Vault Introducing 'Haejwo Local', a local orchestrator for Windows that coordinates multiple AI coding agents and protects authentication credentials via a secure credential vault Distributes tasks across various agents including Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity, and Devin based on overarching AI instructions, supporting handoffs between agents Features a DPAPI and AES-256-GCM-based credential vault to inject environment variables and mask sensitive data, preventing accounts and API keys from being directly exposed to agents Supports session/account switching via account pooling and usage forecasting, task continuation, and automation to minimize user intervention Show GN: buttonmasher – A Claude Code skill to find duplicate payment and retry bugs An article introducing buttonmasher, a Claude Code skill for detecting duplicate payment and retry bugs. buttonmasher, a dedicated skill for Claude Code designed to detect duplicate payments and bugs occurring during retries, has been released. Detailed implementation and usage could not be verified because the provided body text was replaced with a notice requesting JavaScript and cookie verification. Google Announces Gemini Agent for Work — Long-Running Tasks, Memory Retention, and Claude Support Google has announced a Gemini agent for enterprise work featuring long-running task execution, memory retention, and Claude support. Google unveiled a new Gemini-powered agent designed to boost work productivity Provides the ability to handle complex tasks over extended periods and retain long-term memory Includes integration and support for Claude models World Action Models vs VLAs: Robotics Just Had Its GPT-2 Moment [N] An article discussing how robotics has reached a GPT-2-like turning point by comparing World Action Models (WAMs) and Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models in the field of robotics. Compares and analyzes the approaches between World Action Models (WAMs) and Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models. Suggests that current advancements in robotics have reached a turning point analogous to the emergence of GPT-2 in the field of natural language processing. Detailed technical discussions are difficult to determine as almost no main text was provided. Has machine learning research gotten more \"wordy\"? [D] A researcher raises concerns that recent machine learning research papers have become excessively narrative-heavy and long, while lacking mathematical rigor, leading to reduced readability. There is a growing trend where recent machine learning papers—not only in the LLM field but also in other areas like optimization—become excessively lengthy, reaching 20 to 40 pages. The increased use of colloquial expressions and undefined neologisms compromises academic rigor and impairs paper readability. An overreliance on detail-lacking block diagrams instead of mathematical explanations makes practical implementation in code or mathematical formulas difficult. Sliced Wasserstein embeddings + HNSW for color-scheme search over 48k paintings [P] Introduction to Palette Atlas, a project that implements ultra-fast color scheme search across over 48,000 public domain master paintings using Sliced Wasserstein embeddings and HNSW. Constructed 1D W1-based embedding vectors by reducing painting images to 128 colors, projecting them onto 8 directions on a Fibonacci hemisphere, and averaging them across 16 quantiles. Achieved a 78% top-10 match rate compared to exact EMD across 240 queries, demonstrating significantly superior accuracy over conventional soft histograms or mean color methods. Reduced search latency to 0.25 ms compared to 14 ms for brute force, achieving a recall@10 of 1.000 at ef=32 using a custom HNSW index implemented from scratch. I trained a 414k-parameter transformer to fly a boids flock, then tested whether the rules a probe can read are the ones it uses [P] This study explores the working mechanism of a 414k-parameter transformer that learned Boids flocking flight without prior knowledge of the rules, and investigates internal rule utilization via linear probes. Trained on a laptop CPU, a 414k-parameter transformer with two attention layers predicted flight with high accuracy (R² 0.990 to 0.994) on unseen clips and generalized up to 50 birds after being trained on only 12. Linear probe analysis revealed that the cohesion rule could be read even before training (R² approx. 0.85), while the alignment rule was identified after the first attention layer. Early models cheated by finding answers purely from the past 8-tick history without referencing flock information, raising questions about causal intervention methods that restrict attention in individual layers. Do I need to do anything now to use an ARR August review for NAACL 2027? [D] This is an inquiry asking whether any prior action is needed to submit a paper to NAACL 2027 using reviews from the ARR August 2026 cycle. The author submitted a paper to ARR for the first time and has a reviewed paper from the August 2026 cycle. Considering that ARR reviews remain valid for 6 months, the author wants to submit to NAACL 2027 instead of EACL. The author seeks to confirm whether they just need to wait until the NAACL submission site opens or if prior action is required regarding the current August submission. Context Language Models: Self-Managing Context to Improve Performance and Reduce Compute Costs Researchers from Meta, MIT, and the University of Washington proposed 'Context Language Models (CLM)', in which language models edit and manage their own context without external rules to boost performance and reduce computational costs. Instead of conventional summarization, compression, or external retrieval, context is treated like a modifiable file, enabling the model to autonomously rewrite previous messages and remove unnecessary information. Through three approaches—zero-shot, in-context learning, and reinforcement learning—they demonstrated that models can learn distinct context management strategies that surpass human-engineered rules. While significantly reducing computational costs (FLOPs) and improving accuracy on benchmarks such as BrowseComp-Plus and EdgeBench, challenges remain regarding safety risks like information loss and the persistence of prompt injections. Anthropic Temporarily Suspends Free 'Claude for Startups' Perks… Full Review Following Surge in Applications Following the announcement of expanding its startup support program, Anthropic has temporarily suspended the provision of free subscriptions and API credits and initiated a full review due to an overwhelming surge in applications. Anthropic temporarily suspended the provision of one-year Claude Team subscriptions for up to 5 users and $1,000 in API credits for new applicants due to reaching capacity limits. Within 48 hours of announcing the program expansion, hundreds of thousands of applications poured in, amounting to 21 times the volume received over the previous 5 months. As Anthropic overhauls approval criteria and conducts a comprehensive review of existing submissions, some approved statuses may be revoked, sparking pushback from applicants. \"Talk to AI Like a Colleague at Work\"... Prompting Advice from Claude Code Developer Claude Code developer Boris Cherny advised that instead of complex instructions, prompts should be structured around goals, expected standards, and verification criteria, as if conversing with a coworker. Due to advancements in AI model performance, the need for excessively detailed and rigid prompt writing has decreased. The key to prompt writing is clearly presenting three elements: what you want to create, the level of effort to invest, and how to verify the results. Use cases of Anthropic's latest model 'Opus 5.5', reasoning effort configuration (setting to medium), and feedback on session usage issues were also mentioned. Arena Secures $200M Investment to Introduce 'Alignment Index' for AI Safety Evaluation AI model evaluation platform Arena has secured approximately $200 million (about 270 billion KRW) in Series B funding, reaching a valuation of $3.1 billion (about 4 trillion KRW), and is expanding its business into the 'Alignment Index' domain to evaluate AI safety. Arena raised $200 million in a round led by Lightspeed Venture Partners and Khosla Ventures, achieving a valuation of $3.1 billion. Leveraging the investment, Arena plans to move beyond simple performance comparisons to introduce an 'Alignment Index' verifying AI safety and reliability, while strengthening tailored enterprise evaluation services. In preliminary alignment rankings calculated based on conversation data from Agent Arena, GPT-6.1 Sol ranked first, followed by Claude Opus 5.5 in second place. [Contribution] Korea Deep Learning: \"3 Out of 4 Companies Invest in AI Data... Now 'AI-Ready' Alone Is Not Enough\" As AI moves beyond merely answering questions to executing real tasks, corporate data preparation standards must evolve from simply 'AI-ready' to 'agent-ready' data that supports judgment and action. Beyond data quality management at a level simply understood by LLMs, an 'agent-ready data' framework is required so agents can assess task states and decide next actions. For agents to carry out tasks, rather than merely extracting values from documents and structured data, data context, origins, verification results, and business rules must be organically connected. The data management system must continuously support re-verifying state changes and tracking the rationale behind decisions whenever business rules or review criteria change. Is Anthropic Developing Its Own Voice Model? 'Voice' Menu Spotted on Claude Web Signs suggest Anthropic is preparing to enhance its voice capabilities by adding a 'Voice' preview menu to the Claude web interface and introducing an opt-in setting for training data collection. According to TestingCatalog, a 'Voice' Preview item—presumed to be an internal testing environment—was discovered in the Claude web navigation menu. Anthropic recently added an opt-in setting in user preferences allowing voice conversation data to be used to train and improve AI models. Attention is focused on whether this move aims to expand its voice service, previously limited to auxiliary features amid reports of utilizing ElevenLabs technology, toward proprietary model development or an advanced voice interface. HPE Unveils 4 AI Servers Powered by AMD's Next-Generation CPUs, Covering Everything from Inference to Security HPE has unveiled four ProLiant Gen13 servers equipped with 6th Gen AMD EPYC processors, optimized for AI inference and enterprise workloads. HPE announced four ProLiant Gen13 servers (DL585a, DL525, XD245, XD285) based on the 6th Gen AMD EPYC 9006 series, targeting AI inference and enterprise infrastructure. The DL585a is a high-density server supporting up to 8 GPUs, forming a workload-tailored lineup alongside direct liquid-cooled (XD245) and air-cooled (XD285) high-efficiency models and a 1U single-socket model (DL525). Through iLO 8 server management technology, enterprise security and operational efficiency have been significantly reinforced with default data encryption, multi-user authorization, and post-quantum cryptography (PQC) communication extension. \"Expanding SIEM Monitoring to Generative AI\"... Logpresso Launches Integration App Logpresso supports generative AI security monitoring by launching an integration app that consolidates and analyzes ChatGPT Enterprise audit logs with its SIEM platform. Logpresso launches a 'ChatGPT Enterprise' integration app that connects with OpenAI's ChatGPT Compliance API to collect and analyze 10 types of audit logs, including conversation messages, account authentication, and MCP calls Enables detection and tracking of confidential data leaks, anomalous activities, and internal security policy violations through cross-analysis with existing security data Supports reviewing audit logs in the form of actual ChatGPT conversation flows with Markdown formatting applied through a dedicated inquiry view in 'Logpresso Sonar 5.0'"